/* WISEBYTE LESSONS · life everyday (Australia) */
WB.L("medicare-and-finding-a-gp", "life", "beginner", "Medicare and finding a GP you trust",
  "Most Australians carry a green card in their wallet without really knowing what it does. Here is how Medicare works in practice, and how to find a doctor who will know you by name.",
  [
    ["What Medicare covers", "Medicare pays a rebate for many out-of-hospital services and covers public patients in public hospitals at no cost.",
      "Picture two people seeing the same GP on the same morning. One walks out paying nothing, the other pays at the desk and gets part of it back a day later. Both are using Medicare, Australia's public health scheme.\n\nIt pays a rebate, based on a government-set fee, for a long list of out-of-hospital services such as GP visits, many specialist consultations, blood tests and scans. It also covers treatment as a public patient in a public hospital at no cost. It does not cover most dental care, glasses, ambulance trips in most states or private hospital accommodation. This is general information; details are at servicesaustralia.gov.au."],
    ["Cards, apps and bulk billing", "Link Medicare to myGov to manage your card and rebates, and ask any clinic whether it bulk bills before you book.",
      "Citizens and permanent residents can enrol, as can some visitors from countries with reciprocal agreements. Most Australians start on a parent's card, and you can apply for your own from age 15. Linking Medicare to myGov lets you see claims, update bank details so rebates land in your account, and download a digital card. If a clinic charges upfront, the rebate is often paid back within minutes.\n\nWhen a GP bulk bills, they accept the rebate as full payment and you pay nothing. Otherwise you pay the gap between their fee and the rebate. Gaps vary widely, and some practices bulk bill only certain groups, such as children or concession card holders. So ask first: 'Do you bulk bill, and if not, what will I be out of pocket for a standard consult?'"],
    ["The safety net", "After a costly year of out-of-pocket expenses, the Medicare Safety Net can raise your rebates, but couples and families must register together.",
      "Some years are expensive. A run of specialist visits, scans and tests can add up quickly, especially for out-of-hospital care. The Medicare Safety Net is designed for this. Once your gap payments for eligible services pass a threshold in a calendar year, Medicare pays a higher rebate for the rest of that year.\n\nIndividuals are registered automatically, but couples and families need to register together so their costs are combined, and many people forget. You can do this through your Medicare online account in myGov. Thresholds change each year, so check current figures on the Services Australia website rather than relying on an old number you heard from a friend."],
    ["A GP who knows you", "Seeing the same GP over time means better care, so choose one who listens and explains clearly.",
      "A GP who has seen you for years remembers that your blood pressure crept up last winter and that your father had heart trouble. That continuity is linked with better management of long-term conditions and fewer unnecessary tests. MyMedicare is a voluntary scheme where you register with one practice, which can unlock longer telehealth consultations. Even without registering, stick with one clinic and, where possible, one doctor, and ask for a longer appointment when you have several issues.\n\nTo find a good one, try the healthdirect Service Finder, which lists nearby practices, hours and bulk billing, and ask friends what they like. Notice whether the GP listens without rushing and explains things plainly. If the fit is wrong, move on; your old practice can transfer your records."],
    ["After hours and emergencies", "Know your options outside clinic hours, and call triple zero (000) in an emergency.",
      "Illness rarely keeps business hours. The healthdirect helpline on 1800 022 222 connects you with a registered nurse, who can help you decide whether to see a GP, go to an urgent care clinic or head to hospital.\n\nMany areas now have Medicare Urgent Care Clinics, which treat urgent but not life-threatening problems, such as minor fractures or cuts needing stitches, often without an appointment and without a fee. Telehealth GP services can handle many simple issues by phone or video. For chest pain, trouble breathing, signs of stroke or serious injury, call triple zero (000) straight away. Save these options in your phone now so you are not searching in a panic later."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Which is covered by Medicare for most people?", ["Most routine dental check-ups and cleaning","Glasses and contact lenses for adults","Public patient care in a public hospital","Private hospital room and accommodation fees"], 2, "Medicare covers public patients in public hospitals, plus rebates on many out-of-hospital services."],
    [1, "What does it mean when a GP bulk bills?", ["They add a surcharge that Medicare repays later","They accept the rebate as full payment, so you pay nothing","They charge their own fee and Medicare refunds it in full","They only treat patients who hold concession cards"], 1, "Bulk billing means the rebate is the whole payment; otherwise you pay the gap, so ask before you book."],
    [2, "How should couples and families use the Medicare Safety Net?", ["Apply separately each month with receipts","Wait until the next calendar year to claim","Register together so their costs are combined","Do nothing, since couples are always combined automatically"], 2, "Individuals are registered automatically, but families must register together to combine costs."],
    [3, "Why is it worth sticking with one regular GP?", ["They know your history, which supports better long-term care","Medicare pays a higher rebate for repeat visits to one GP","Seeing several different doctors lowers your gap fees","Only regular patients are allowed to use telehealth"], 0, "Continuity of care helps a GP spot changes early and manage long-term conditions."],
    [4, "Which situation calls for triple zero (000)?", ["A minor fracture on a quiet Sunday","Wondering whether a mild cough needs a GP","A routine health question after clinic hours","Chest pain or trouble breathing"], 3, "Serious symptoms need an ambulance; nurses and urgent care clinics handle less severe problems."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Same GP, same morning: one patient pays nothing, the other pays then is refunded."});

WB.L("mygov-and-government-services", "life", "beginner", "myGov without the headache",
  "One login for Centrelink, Medicare, the tax office and more sounds simple, until you are stuck on hold. Here is how to make the system work for you.",
  [
    ["One front door", "myGov is one secure login that reaches many federal services, each of which still holds its own records.",
      "Imagine you have just moved house. Once, you might have filled in separate forms for the tax office, Medicare and Centrelink. With myGov, you sign in once at my.gov.au and update your details for several services together.\n\nmyGov is not a service itself, more like a front door with a set of keys. Behind it sit linked services such as the Australian Taxation Office, Medicare, Centrelink, Child Support, My Health Record and the Department of Veterans' Affairs, along with some state services. You choose which ones to link. Each service still holds its own records. Linking just lets you reach them all from one place and receive their letters in one inbox."],
    ["Setting up and linking", "Use your own email and a strong sign-in, then link services by answering questions only you should know.",
      "Type my.gov.au yourself and create an account with an email address only you can access, not a shared family email or a work address you might lose. Choose a sign-in method such as a text code, an authenticator app, a passkey or the myID app; stronger options make break-ins harder. Each adult should have their own account, even couples who share everything else.\n\nLinking is where many people get stuck. The ATO may ask for details from a past tax return, Medicare for your card number and recent claim, and Centrelink can give you a linking code if you call or visit. These checks stop impostors. Gather a payslip, tax file number, Medicare card, ID such as a licence or passport, and agency letters, then do it in one sitting at a desk."],
    ["Inbox and scams", "Check your inbox for deadlines, and never sign in through a link in a text or email: genuine myGov messages do not ask you to.",
      "Many linked services now send letters to your myGov inbox instead of the post, including notices about payments, debts and tax, some with deadlines. Missing one can be costly: if Centrelink asks you to confirm your income and you do not reply, your payment can be paused. Turn on notifications, read letters in full and save important ones as PDFs.\n\nScammers also copy myGov. A text may say you are owed a refund or your account is suspended, with a link to a fake site that steals your login. Services Australia has said myGov texts and emails will not contain links asking you to sign in. Close the message and type my.gov.au yourself, or use the official app. If you entered details on a fake page, change your password and contact Services Australia. Report scams to Scamwatch (scamwatch.gov.au)."],
    ["Getting through to a person", "Do routine tasks online, and prepare before you call so one call does the job.",
      "Most routine tasks, such as updating an address, reporting income or claiming a Medicare rebate, can be done online or in the app, often faster than waiting on the phone.\n\nWhen you do need a person, prepare before you dial. Have your customer reference number, your myGov sign-in, and a short note of what you need and what has happened so far. Calls are usually quieter early in the morning and later in the week, though this varies. Write down the date, the name or identifier of the person you spoke to and what they said. Service centres can help in person, and Services Australia also offers interpreter services and support for people who are not comfortable online."],
    ["When things go wrong", "If you disagree with a decision, ask for an explanation, then a formal review, and keep records.",
      "Government decisions are not always right. If Centrelink cuts a payment or raises a debt you think is wrong, first ask for an explanation of how the decision was made. You can then request a formal review, where someone not involved in the original decision looks again. Time limits can apply, especially if you want any back pay, so act quickly.\n\nBeyond that, independent bodies such as the Administrative Review Tribunal can review many decisions, and the Commonwealth Ombudsman investigates complaints about how agencies have treated you. Free legal help is available through community legal centres and Economic Justice Australia's network. This is general information; check the details for your situation on servicesaustralia.gov.au."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is myGov?", ["A government agency that holds all your records itself","A single login that links services, which still hold their own records","A tax service run only by the ATO","A replacement for Medicare and Centrelink"], 1, "myGov is a front door: one account to reach services that keep their own records."],
    [1, "Why do services ask questions when you link them to myGov?", ["To collect extra data for marketing","To stop impostors, using details only you should know","To check that your payments are up to date","To confirm you have lodged a tax return"], 1, "The checks protect you by making it hard for someone else to link to your records."],
    [2, "A text about myGov includes a link to sign in. What should you do?", ["Sign in quickly in case your account is suspended","Reply to ask whether the message is genuine","Ignore the link and type my.gov.au into your browser yourself","Forward it to friends so they can check it"], 2, "Genuine myGov messages will not ask you to sign in through a link, so go to the site yourself."],
    [3, "How can you make a call to Services Australia more effective?", ["Skip online options so staff can handle everything","Have your details ready and note who you spoke to","Call at midday and ask for a manager first","Wait to explain the problem until you reach an operator"], 1, "Preparing before you call, and keeping notes, means one call is more likely to do the job."],
    [4, "If you think Centrelink has made a wrong decision, what is a sensible first step?", ["Ask for an explanation of how the decision was made","Go straight to the Commonwealth Ombudsman","Wait until the time limit has passed, then appeal","Lodge a new claim and ignore the decision"], 0, "Ask for an explanation first, then a formal review, and act quickly because time limits can apply."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"myGov will never text you a sign-in link. Here is how to spot the fakes."});

WB.L("buying-a-used-car", "life", "intermediate", "Buying a used car without getting burned",
  "A shiny paint job can hide a lot. With a few checks and a cool head, you can buy a second hand car that is exactly what it seems.",
  [
    ["Decide before you browse", "Set a total budget, including extra costs, and a shortlist of reliable models before you look at any listing.",
      "Scrolling car listings late at night is a fast way to fall in love with the wrong vehicle. Start with a plan. Work out what the car must do: daily commuting, towing, long country drives or squeezing into tight city parks.\n\nSet a total budget that includes transfer costs, stamp duty, insurance and a buffer for early repairs, not just the sticker price. Research a shortlist of reliable models using independent reviews and safety ratings from ANCAP, the Australasian New Car Assessment Program. Check running costs such as fuel use, servicing and insurance premiums too. Once three or four models suit you, every listing becomes easier to judge calmly."],
    ["Protections and the PPSR", "Dealers give more protection than private sellers, and a PPSR search shows whether a car has money owing, is written off or is stolen.",
      "Licensed dealers usually charge more, but you get protection. In several states they must provide a statutory warranty on cars under a certain age and kilometres, some states give a short cooling-off period, and Australian Consumer Law guarantees apply. Private sales are often cheaper, but the car is largely sold as it is, with generally no cooling-off period. Auctions can offer bargains, but you usually cannot test drive and recourse is limited. Rules differ by state, so check your fair trading website.\n\nWhatever the channel, search the Personal Property Securities Register at ppsr.gov.au using the car's VIN. For a small fee, it shows whether a lender has a security interest (and could repossess the car after you buy) and whether it is recorded as written off or stolen. Search on the day you pay and save the certificate."],
    ["Inspect and test drive", "View the car in daylight, start it cold and drive it properly, testing everything from brakes to warning lights.",
      "View the car in daylight, at the seller's home if it is a private sale. Ask the seller not to start the engine before you arrive: a cold start can reveal rattles, smoke and hard starting. Look for uneven panel gaps or mismatched paint, which can point to accident repairs. Check tyre wear, under the oil cap for milky residue, and under the car for leaks. Check the service book, and that the VIN matches the car and the registration name matches the seller's licence.\n\nThen drive for at least twenty minutes on suburban streets, a highway and a hill, with the radio off. Listen for knocks and squealing, check braking and gear changes, and test every switch. Warning lights should come on briefly at start-up then go out. If anything feels rushed or evasive, walk away."],
    ["Get a professional opinion", "An independent pre-purchase inspection is cheap insurance on an expensive purchase, and a useful negotiating tool.",
      "Even confident car people miss things. An independent pre-purchase inspection, from a mechanic you choose or a motoring club such as the NRMA, RACV or RACQ, typically costs a few hundred dollars. It can uncover rust, hidden accident damage, worn suspension or engine problems.\n\nThe report is also a powerful negotiating tool: if it finds worn brake pads and an overdue timing belt, you can reasonably ask for the cost to come off the price. Never let the seller choose the mechanic. If a seller refuses to allow an inspection, treat that as a red flag. Also check the registration status and what safety or roadworthy certificate your state requires before transfer."],
    ["Pay and transfer safely", "Never pay for a car you have not inspected, pay by traceable means, get a receipt and lodge the transfer promptly.",
      "Online car scams often follow a pattern: a great price, a seller who is overseas or in the army, and a request for a deposit before you can see the car. Never pay for a car you have not inspected in person.\n\nWhen you are ready to buy, pay by bank transfer or bank cheque rather than carrying large amounts of cash. Get a written receipt with both names, the date, the price, the VIN and the registration number. Both buyer and seller usually must complete a transfer form, and you will pay stamp duty and a transfer fee within a set number of days. Arrange insurance before you drive away, not the next morning."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What should a used-car budget include?", ["Only the advertised price, with extras sorted later","Transfer costs, stamp duty, insurance and a buffer for early repairs","The advertised price plus the expected resale value","Fuel costs for the first year only"], 1, "A total budget covers on-road costs and repairs, not just the sticker price."],
    [1, "What can a PPSR search reveal?", ["The car's full service history","Whether the seller has a clean driving record","Money owing on the car, plus any write-off or stolen record","The price similar cars have recently sold for"], 2, "A PPSR certificate protects you from buying a car a lender could repossess or that has a hidden history."],
    [2, "Why ask the seller not to start the engine before you arrive?", ["To keep the battery charged for the test drive","A cold start can reveal rattles and smoke","It shows how much fuel the car uses","Warm engines fail registration checks"], 1, "Problems hide in a warm engine, so seeing a cold start gives you a more honest picture."],
    [3, "Why get an independent pre-purchase inspection?", ["It is legally required before every transfer","It replaces the need for a PPSR search","It can uncover faults and help you negotiate","It guarantees the car will never need repairs"], 2, "A mechanic you choose can find faults you missed, and the report helps you bargain."],
    [4, "What is the safest way to pay for a used car?", ["Send a deposit before viewing to hold the car","Hand over a large sum in cash with no receipt","Pay an overseas seller through a third-party agent","After inspecting it, pay by bank transfer or cheque and get a receipt"], 3, "Inspect first, pay traceably and keep a receipt with names, price, VIN and registration."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"A bargain car can still belong to a finance company. How to check before you pay."});

WB.L("travelling-overseas-smoothly", "life", "beginner", "Travelling overseas without the drama",
  "The best trips start long before the airport. A little preparation with your passport, the right insurance and official travel advice can save you a world of trouble.",
  [
    ["Passport and entry rules", "Check your passport expiry months ahead, as many countries want six months' validity, and check entry rules for every country, including transits.",
      "Every year some travellers are turned away at check-in because their passport expires too soon. Many countries require it to be valid for at least six months beyond your arrival or departure date, and some need blank pages. Check the expiry date months ahead and renew at passports.gov.au. Processing times can stretch in busy seasons, and priority processing costs extra.\n\nAustralians can visit many countries without a visa, but not all. Some require an electronic authorisation applied for online, such as the US ESTA or UK ETA; others need an embassy visa. Do not forget countries where you only change planes. Use official government sites linked from Smartraveller, and beware look-alike sites charging large fees for forms you could lodge yourself. Some common medicines are restricted overseas, so check those rules too."],
    ["Smartraveller and insurance", "Read Smartraveller's advice for every destination, and buy travel insurance when you book, checking what it excludes.",
      "Smartraveller (smartraveller.gov.au), run by the Department of Foreign Affairs and Trade, gives each destination an advice level, from 'Exercise normal safety precautions' up to 'Do not travel'. Regions can differ. Read it before you book and again before you leave. Insurers may refuse cover in places marked 'Do not travel'.\n\nSmartraveller puts it bluntly: if you cannot afford travel insurance, you cannot afford to travel. Overseas treatment can cost tens of thousands of dollars, and an evacuation flight home far more. Buy cover when you book so cancellation is protected, then read the product disclosure statement for pre-existing conditions, the excess, and exclusions such as scooter riding without the right licence, extreme sports or alcohol. Credit card insurance often has strict conditions."],
    ["Money, phones, documents", "Carry two ways to pay, sort out phone roaming, and keep copies of key documents where you can reach them.",
      "A card blocked for suspicious overseas activity can leave you stranded at a hotel desk. Check whether your bank wants to know your travel plans, and carry at least two ways to pay, kept in different places. Compare fees: some cards charge international transaction fees on every purchase, while travel cards and some bank accounts do not.\n\nBefore you leave, check your phone plan's roaming charges, which can be huge, or plan to buy a local SIM or eSIM. Save digital copies of your passport, insurance policy and bookings somewhere you can reach from any device, and share them with someone at home. A printed copy of your insurer's emergency number in your wallet is a smart backup."],
    ["Your health on the road", "See a GP or travel clinic six to eight weeks before departure for vaccinations and medicine advice.",
      "Some vaccinations need several weeks to take effect, or more than one dose, so book a GP or travel clinic six to eight weeks before departure if you can. Bring a list of your medicines and a letter from your doctor describing them, and keep them in original labelled packaging.\n\nAustralia has reciprocal health care agreements with some countries, including the United Kingdom, New Zealand and Ireland. These can cover medically necessary treatment, but they are limited and do not replace insurance. On long flights, move around and drink water. Healthdirect and Smartraveller both have practical travel health pages. This is general information, not medical advice, so talk to a health professional about your own needs."],
    ["When things go wrong", "The Australian government can help in a crisis, but it cannot pay your bills or override local laws, so insurance and preparation matter.",
      "If your passport is stolen, report it to local police and contact the nearest Australian embassy or consulate for an emergency travel document. If you are arrested, seriously ill or caught in a crisis, consular staff can help, and the 24-hour Consular Emergency Centre can be reached from anywhere. Smartraveller lists the numbers.\n\nBut the Consular Services Charter is clear about the limits. The government cannot pay your medical bills or legal fees, get you out of jail or override local laws. That is exactly why insurance and good preparation matter. Before you leave, make sure someone at home has your itinerary and knows how to reach you, and check in with them regularly."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why check your passport expiry date months before travelling?", ["Passports expire automatically when you leave Australia","Many countries want six months' validity beyond your trip","Airlines refuse any passport that contains stamps","Visas cannot be issued in the final year of validity"], 1, "Short validity can see you refused at check-in, so check early and leave time to renew."],
    [1, "What does a 'Do not travel' level mean for your travel insurance?", ["Cover automatically doubles","Your policy is unaffected","Cancellation is guaranteed regardless of your policy","Insurers may refuse to cover you there"], 3, "Smartraveller's level matters for your policy, as insurers may not cover places marked 'Do not travel'."],
    [2, "How can you avoid being stranded by a blocked card overseas?", ["Carry all your cards and cash together for easy access","Carry two ways to pay in different places and tell your bank if needed","Rely on roaming data to sort out bank problems","Use a single card and keep a spare PIN written down"], 1, "Backup payment methods, kept separately, protect you if one card is blocked."],
    [3, "When should you see a GP or travel clinic before an overseas trip?", ["On the day before departure","Six to eight weeks ahead, as some vaccines need time or extra doses","After arrival, if you feel unwell","Only if the destination is marked 'Do not travel'"], 1, "Vaccinations can need several weeks, so book early."],
    [4, "What can the Australian government do for Australians in trouble overseas?", ["Pay your medical bills if you have no insurance cover","Override local laws to get you released from custody","Cover your legal fees if you are arrested abroad","Offer emergency help, but not pay bills or override laws"], 3, "Consular help has limits, which is why insurance and preparation matter."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your passport can be valid today and still be refused at the airport. Why?"});

WB.L("making-a-will", "life", "intermediate", "Making a will, and why it matters",
  "Nobody likes thinking about it, but a will is one of the kindest things you can leave the people you love. Here is what it does and how to get one right.",
  [
    ["Why you need one", "Without a valid will, law decides who gets what by formula; a will names your beneficiaries, executor and guardian for children.",
      "Imagine a couple who have lived together for years but never married, with a child from an earlier relationship. One dies suddenly without a will. The partner may face a slow, stressful process to prove the relationship, while assets are divided by a formula in state or territory law, not by what the person wanted. This is dying intestate. The rules favour spouses, partners, children and close relatives in a set order, and do not cover friends, charities or stepchildren as many expect.\n\nA will sets out what happens to your estate. It names beneficiaries and what each receives, including specific gifts, such as a ring to a niece, and the 'residue', which is everything else. It names an executor to gather assets, pay debts and distribute what is left, and a guardian for children under 18."],
    ["Choosing an executor", "Pick someone trustworthy and organised, and ask them first, because the role is real work.",
      "Being an executor is not an honour to frame on the wall. It can mean months of paperwork: locating documents, dealing with banks, possibly applying to the Supreme Court for probate, selling property and settling tax.\n\nChoose someone you trust, who is organised and likely to outlive you, and ask them before naming them. Many people pick a partner or adult child, often with a backup in case the first cannot act. Some appoint a professional, such as a solicitor or the state trustee organisation, which charges fees but brings experience and neutrality. Naming two people who do not get along as joint executors can cause exactly the conflict you hoped to avoid."],
    ["Making it valid", "A will generally needs to be in writing, signed by you and witnessed by two independent adults, and a solicitor is usually money well spent.",
      "The formal rules vary a little between states and territories, but the core is similar. The will must be in writing, signed by you, and signed by two adult witnesses who are both present when you sign. A witness who is also a beneficiary, or a beneficiary's partner, can create problems and the gift to them may be at risk in some places, so use independent witnesses. Courts can sometimes accept informal documents, but proving them is slow and expensive.\n\nDo-it-yourself kits can work for very simple situations, yet small mistakes are common. A solicitor is money well spent with blended families, a business, overseas assets or a family member who may contest. Each state and territory also has a public trustee that can prepare wills, sometimes at low cost. This is general information, not legal advice."],
    ["What a will misses", "Superannuation and jointly owned assets often pass outside your will, so plan them separately.",
      "Many Australians are surprised that their superannuation does not automatically follow their will. Super is held in trust, and the fund trustee decides who receives it unless you have made a valid binding death benefit nomination. Some binding nominations lapse after a few years unless renewed, so check with your fund. Life insurance held inside super follows the same path.\n\nProperty owned as joint tenants, such as a home held jointly by a couple, usually passes straight to the surviving owner regardless of the will. Bank accounts held jointly can work the same way. Review these alongside your will, or your careful plan may cover only a fraction of what you own."],
    ["Updates and other documents", "Review your will when life changes, and cover decisions while you are alive with an enduring power of attorney and advance care directive.",
      "Store the original somewhere safe and tell your executor where. Review it whenever life changes. In most states, marrying revokes an existing will unless it was made in contemplation of that marriage, and divorce can cancel gifts to a former spouse. Children, separation, buying property or a beneficiary dying are also reasons to check it.\n\nA will only speaks after death. If illness left you unable to decide, other documents are needed. An enduring power of attorney lets someone you trust manage your financial and legal affairs. An enduring guardianship or similar appointment, with an advance care directive, covers health decisions and records your wishes about treatment. Forms differ between states, so ask a solicitor. Talking with your family is uncomfortable for minutes and a gift for years."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What happens if you die without a valid will?", ["Your executor decides freely who gets what","Your assets pass to the government","State or territory law divides your assets by a fixed formula","Your close friends inherit equally with your family"], 2, "Intestacy rules follow a set order, not your wishes, and can leave out people you care about."],
    [1, "What makes a good executor?", ["Anyone willing, even if you have not asked them","Someone trustworthy and organised, asked in advance","Two people who disagree, so they keep each other honest","A distant relative, whatever their reliability"], 1, "The role involves months of paperwork, so choose carefully and ask first."],
    [2, "What is generally needed for a valid will?", ["Registration with a court before you sign","A video recording in place of a written signature","A single witness who is also a beneficiary","Writing, your signature and two adult witnesses present together"], 3, "Informal documents are slow and expensive to prove, so follow the formal rules and use independent witnesses."],
    [3, "Who usually decides who receives your superannuation?", ["The fund trustee, unless you made a binding nomination","Your executor, who must follow your will exactly","The Australian Taxation Office, after your death","Your surviving spouse, automatically by default"], 0, "Super is held in trust, so it can pass outside your will."],
    [4, "What does an enduring power of attorney cover that a will cannot?", ["Distributing your property to beneficiaries after death","Managing your financial affairs if you cannot decide","Naming a guardian for your children under 18","Cancelling all of your debts when you die"], 1, "A will only speaks after death; other documents cover decisions while you are alive."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your will may not cover your super. Find out what it can't control."});

WB.L("complaints-that-get-results", "life", "advanced", "Making a complaint that actually gets results",
  "Knowing your rights is one thing; getting a business to honour them is another. Here is the step by step path from polite email to independent ombudsman.",
  [
    ["Goal and evidence", "Decide the outcome you want, know your consumer guarantees, and keep evidence and a log.",
      "Consider a washing machine that dies a fortnight after its warranty ends. Angry calls get nowhere; clarity works. Write down what went wrong, when, and what you want: a repair, replacement, refund or compensation. Then check Australian Consumer Law. Its consumer guarantees mean goods must be of acceptable quality and last a reasonable time, regardless of any manufacturer's warranty, and a major failure gives you a choice of remedy. The ACCC (accc.gov.au) explains this in plain English.\n\nGather proof of purchase, photos or video of the fault, and any packaging. Start a log of every call, email and visit, with the date, the person and what they said or promised, including any sales claims. It makes your complaint precise, and shows you tried to resolve things first. This is general information, not legal advice."],
    ["Complain in writing", "Contact the business first, then follow up in writing with a clear remedy and deadline.",
      "Most problems are fixed at the first step, so give the business a genuine chance. Ask to speak with someone who can make decisions, often a manager or the complaints team rather than front counter staff. Be polite, specific and firm.\n\nIf that does not work, put your complaint in writing by email or letter. Briefly set out what you bought, the problem, what you have done so far and the remedy you want. Refer to the consumer guarantees if relevant, and give a reasonable deadline, such as 14 days, for a response. Many large businesses must have an internal dispute resolution process, and asking for your complaint to be formally logged often moves it to more senior people."],
    ["Fair trading and ombudsmen", "If the business will not budge, escalate to your state fair trading agency or a free industry ombudsman.",
      "For most goods and services, contact your state fair trading agency, such as NSW Fair Trading or Consumer Affairs Victoria. They can advise and may contact the business for you. The ACCC enforces the law nationally but generally does not resolve individual disputes.\n\nSeveral industries have free, independent ombudsman schemes: the Telecommunications Industry Ombudsman for phone and internet, the Australian Financial Complaints Authority for banks, insurers and super funds, and state energy and water ombudsmen. Most require you to complain to the business first. The scheme sends your complaint back to senior staff for a last chance to fix it, and many settle there. If not, it investigates and may recommend or decide an outcome. With AFCA, a decision you accept can bind the business, and you can still go to court if you reject it."],
    ["Tribunals and chargebacks", "Small claims tribunals and card chargebacks are powerful tools when other steps fail.",
      "If no ombudsman covers the business and the fair trading agency cannot resolve it, a state tribunal or local court can hear consumer claims. Examples include NCAT in New South Wales, VCAT in Victoria and QCAT in Queensland. Small claims processes are designed to be cheaper and less formal than ordinary courts, though there is usually a filing fee and you present your own case with your evidence.\n\nSeparately, if you paid by credit or debit card and the goods never arrived or the business has collapsed, ask your bank about a chargeback. Card schemes have rules for disputed transactions, and banks set time limits for requests, so contact them as soon as you realise there is a problem."],
    ["Tone and persistence", "Stay calm, factual and persistent, and be open to a fair compromise.",
      "The most effective complainers are rarely the loudest. Staff who are shouted at tend to follow the script; staff who are treated respectfully often look for a way to help. Keep messages short and factual, repeat your requested remedy clearly, and do not accept vague promises without a date.\n\nIf you are offered less than you asked for, consider whether it is fair rather than rejecting it on principle, since time and stress have costs too. Public reviews and social media posts can attract attention, but stick to accurate facts to avoid defamation risks. Finally, report scams and misleading conduct to the ACCC or Scamwatch, even if you get your money back. It helps protect the next customer."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What do consumer guarantees mean for a product that fails soon after its warranty ends?", ["Your rights end when the manufacturer's warranty does","Goods must be of acceptable quality and last a reasonable time","You can only claim compensation, never a refund","Only the manufacturer must fix it, not the retailer"], 1, "Consumer guarantees apply regardless of the manufacturer's warranty, so know them before you complain."],
    [1, "Why follow up in writing after speaking with the business?", ["It lets you skip the conversation with a manager","It guarantees a refund within 14 days","It makes the business pay your costs automatically","It creates a record and sets out a remedy and deadline"], 3, "A written complaint is precise, shows what you tried and gives you a record to rely on later."],
    [2, "When can you take a complaint to an industry ombudsman?", ["Before contacting the business, to save time","Only if you hire a lawyer","After first complaining to the business, at no cost","Only after paying a fee refunded if you win"], 2, "Ombudsman schemes are free and independent, but most expect you to try the business first."],
    [3, "What can you do if goods paid for by card never arrived and the business collapsed?", ["Ask your bank about a chargeback, as soon as you notice","Wait for the collapsed business to contact you","Lodge a claim with the ACCC to recover your money","Ignore it, since card payments cannot be disputed"], 0, "Banks have rules for disputed card transactions and time limits, so act quickly."],
    [4, "What approach tends to get the best result from staff?", ["Loud and emotional, so staff feel pressure","Calm, factual and persistent, with a clearly stated remedy","Accepting any vague promise to end it quickly","Posting accusations on social media first"], 1, "Respectful, specific persistence gets staff looking for ways to help."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your rights may outlast a warranty. How to turn them into a repair or refund."});

WB.L("building-a-better-sleep-routine", "life", "beginner", "Building a better sleep routine",
  "Good sleep is less about a magic pill and more about a handful of habits done consistently. Here is a practical routine you can start tonight.",
  [
    ["Anchor your wake time", "A fixed wake time and morning light are the strongest tools for setting your body clock.",
      "Many people try to fix bad sleep by going to bed earlier, then lie awake staring at the ceiling. A better starting point is the other end of the night. Your body clock, the circadian rhythm, runs on a cycle of roughly 24 hours and is set largely by when you wake and see light.\n\nChoose a wake time you can keep seven days a week, within about half an hour, weekends included. Big Saturday sleep-ins shift your clock later, which is part of why Monday mornings feel brutal. Get outside within an hour of waking, even on a cloudy day. It is a far stronger signal than indoor lighting. In the evening, dim the house lights and lower screen brightness."],
    ["Caffeine, alcohol and dinner", "Caffeine lingers for hours and alcohol fragments sleep, so time both carefully.",
      "That 3 pm flat white may feel harmless, but caffeine stays in your system for several hours, and for some people much longer. It blocks the receptors for adenosine, a chemical that builds up while you are awake and makes you feel sleepy. A common suggestion is to have your last caffeine around midday or early afternoon, and to watch for hidden sources such as energy drinks, cola, some teas and dark chocolate.\n\nAlcohol is trickier because it can make you drowsy at first. Later in the night it tends to disrupt sleep, causing lighter, more broken rest and more trips to the bathroom. Heavy meals close to bedtime can also cause discomfort, so eat dinner a few hours before bed."],
    ["Prepare the night", "A predictable wind-down and a cool, dark, quiet bedroom make sleep easier to start and keep.",
      "Children have bedtime routines for good reason, and adults benefit from the same idea. For the last half hour to an hour, do a few calm things in roughly the same order, such as a warm shower, setting out tomorrow's clothes, reading a paper book and slow breathing. Keep work emails and gripping news out of this window. If worries race, write tomorrow's to-do list first.\n\nThen make the bedroom cool, dark and quiet. In hot Australian summers, a fan, air conditioning or lighter bedding helps, and block-out curtains or an eye mask cut street light and early dawn. Check that your mattress and pillow still support you. Charge your phone outside the room and use a cheap alarm clock instead."],
    ["If you cannot sleep", "If you are awake for a long while, get up and do something calm until you feel sleepy.",
      "Lying in bed for an hour willing yourself to sleep can teach your brain to link bed with frustration. Sleep specialists often suggest a different approach. If you have been awake for what feels like twenty minutes or so, without clock watching, get up and go to another dim room. Do something quiet and boring, such as reading or folding washing, and return to bed only when you feel sleepy. Repeat if needed.\n\nIt feels counterintuitive, but over time it rebuilds the link between bed and sleep. Avoid checking the time repeatedly, which tends to increase anxiety. Keep naps short, ideally under half an hour and before mid afternoon, so they do not steal sleep from the night."],
    ["When to get help", "Ongoing poor sleep, loud snoring or gasping, or daytime sleepiness are reasons to see a GP.",
      "Most adults are thought to need roughly seven to nine hours of sleep, though individuals vary. Occasional bad nights are normal, but persistent trouble is worth taking seriously. See your GP if poor sleep lasts for weeks and affects your daytime life, if a partner notices loud snoring, pauses in breathing or gasping, if you feel very sleepy during the day, especially while driving, or if you have strong urges to move your legs at night. These can point to treatable sleep conditions. Effective help exists, including a structured talking therapy for insomnia known as CBT-I. The Sleep Health Foundation and healthdirect have reliable information. This lesson is general information, not medical advice."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "Why is a steady wake time a better starting point than an earlier bedtime?", ["It anchors your body clock, set by waking and light","It lets you skip the evening wind-down routine","It means weekend sleep-ins no longer matter","It removes any need for morning daylight"], 0, "Your circadian rhythm is set largely by when you wake and see light, so a fixed wake time steadies the whole cycle."],
    [1, "What does alcohol tend to do to sleep later in the night?", ["Deepen it and make it more restful","Make it lighter and more broken","Shorten the time spent dreaming only","Have no effect once you are asleep"], 1, "Alcohol can make you drowsy at first, but it tends to disrupt sleep later, leaving rest lighter and more broken."],
    [2, "If worries race through your head before bed, what helps?", ["Checking work emails to clear them","Reading the news to distract yourself","Writing a quick to-do list for tomorrow first","Lying still until they pass"], 2, "Getting tasks onto paper can make them easier to let go of before you wind down."],
    [3, "What is suggested if you have been awake in bed for what feels like twenty minutes or so?", ["Check the clock to see how long is left","Have a coffee to break the cycle","Scroll your phone in bed until you feel tired","Get up, do something calm, and return when sleepy"], 3, "Leaving bed when you are awake stops your brain linking bed with frustration and rebuilds the link with sleep."],
    [4, "Which of these is a reason to see a GP about sleep?", ["One bad night before an exam","Waking once to use the bathroom","Loud snoring with pauses in breathing","Dreaming vividly now and then"], 2, "Snoring with pauses or gasping can point to a treatable sleep condition, so it is worth a GP visit."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Your Monday morning slump may start with Saturday's sleep-in. Here's how."});

WB.L("private-health-insurance-decoded", "life", "advanced", "Private health insurance, decoded",
  "Gold, Silver, Bronze, extras, excesses and a mysterious loading after 30. Here is how private health insurance really works in Australia, so you can decide what you need.",
  [
    ["Medicare, choice and tax", "Private cover sits on top of Medicare, mainly for choice and timing, and tax rules also nudge people towards it.",
      "Every Australian resident enrolled in Medicare can be treated free as a public patient in a public hospital. So why do close to half of Australians hold private hospital cover? Mostly for choice and timing. As a private patient you can usually choose your specialist and hospital, and elective surgery such as a knee reconstruction may happen sooner than on a public waiting list.\n\nTax rules push some people towards cover as well. The Medicare Levy Surcharge is an extra tax on people above an income threshold who lack appropriate hospital cover. Separately, the Australian Government Rebate reduces premiums for many people, depending on income and age. Check current figures at ato.gov.au. Whether cover is worth it depends on your health, age, income and priorities. This is general information, not financial advice."],
    ["Hospital tiers and extras", "Hospital policies are tiered Gold, Silver, Bronze and Basic by what they must cover, and extras rarely repay the full cost.",
      "Hospital cover pays towards your costs as a private patient, such as accommodation, theatre fees and part of the doctors' fees. Since 2019, policies sit in four tiers: Gold, Silver, Bronze and Basic. Each must cover a minimum list of clinical categories. Gold covers all of them, including pregnancy and birth and joint replacements, while a cheap Basic policy may cover very little. A 'plus' label means extra categories. Compare the treatments you might realistically need, not just the tier name or price.\n\nExtras pays part of the cost of services outside hospital, such as dental, physio and optical. Each service has a benefit amount and an annual limit, so you pay the rest. Add up what you actually spend, then compare it with the premium plus the gaps."],
    ["Excess, gaps, waiting periods", "Know what you pay at the time of treatment and how long you must wait before you can claim.",
      "Two policies in the same tier can cost very different amounts because of the excess, the amount you agree to pay upfront if you are admitted to hospital. A higher excess lowers your premium but raises your bill when you need care.\n\nYou may also face a gap, the difference between what a doctor charges and what Medicare and your insurer pay. Ask your specialist and hospital about likely gaps before treatment, and whether they have an arrangement with your fund.\n\nWaiting periods apply when you first take out cover or upgrade. They are commonly up to 12 months for pre-existing conditions and pregnancy, and shorter for most other hospital treatment. Check the specifics before you need them."],
    ["Lifetime Health Cover loading", "If you take out hospital cover after 30, you may pay a loading of 2 per cent per year over 30 for up to 10 years.",
      "Lifetime Health Cover encourages people to join earlier. If you do not hold private hospital cover by 1 July following your 31st birthday, a loading of 2 per cent is added to your hospital premium for every year you are over 30 when you eventually join, up to a maximum of 70 per cent. Someone first joining at 40 would typically pay around 20 per cent more than someone who joined at 30.\n\nThe loading is removed after you have held hospital cover continuously for 10 years. Some exceptions exist, including for people who arrived in Australia later in life. Extras-only cover does not count towards avoiding the loading."],
    ["Compare and switch", "Use the government's comparison site, compare like with like, and review your cover each year.",
      "Insurers have thousands of policies, and names alone tell you little. The government website privatehealth.gov.au, managed by the Commonwealth Ombudsman, lets you compare policies from all registered funds, and every policy has a standard information statement summarising what it covers. Commercial comparison sites can help, but they may not list every fund.\n\nYou can switch funds. If you move to an equivalent level of hospital cover you generally will not serve the same waiting periods again, though upgrades may attract new ones. Review your cover each year, especially when life changes, such as planning a family or retiring. If you have a dispute with your fund, the Private Health Insurance Ombudsman can help, free of charge. A tax agent can help you apply the tax rules to your situation."]
  ],
  [
    [0, "What is a main reason Australians hold private hospital cover?", ["It is needed to enrol in Medicare","It is compulsory for all residents","Choice of specialist and sooner elective surgery","It removes every cost in public hospitals"], 2, "Medicare already covers public hospital care, so private cover is mainly about choice and timing."],
    [1, "Which hospital tier must cover every clinical category?", ["Gold","Silver","Bronze","Basic"], 0, "Gold must cover all categories, while lower tiers cover fewer, so check the treatments you might need."],
    [2, "What is the effect of choosing a higher excess?", ["No waiting periods","Full cover for extras","Higher premiums and no hospital bill","Lower premiums but a bigger bill when admitted"], 3, "A higher excess trades a cheaper premium for more out-of-pocket cost at the time of care."],
    [3, "Under Lifetime Health Cover, what loading applies for each year over 30 that you wait to take hospital cover?", ["2 per cent","1 per cent","5 per cent","10 per cent"], 0, "The 2 per cent per year loading, capped at 70 per cent, rewards joining earlier and drops away after 10 years of continuous cover."],
    [4, "How can you fairly compare policies from all registered funds?", ["Ask a single fund to rank its rivals","Use the government site privatehealth.gov.au","Compare tier names only","Choose the lowest premium regardless of cover"], 1, "The government site lists all registered funds with standard information statements so you can compare like with like."]
  ],
  {"added":"2026-09-25","push":"Why waiting past 30 to join a health fund can cost you for years after."});

